# Chroma library, modern Earth: THE SPHERES OF SOCIETY, title moments of sphere titles set b. Library thread,
# 2026-10-09, "v22" update stage 4, item 15 (chroma-packs/spheres/title-gaps.md, "The 28 new titles").
# An everyday pair per new title of titles.py (NEW_TITLES) that only its holder meets (holds:), in the style of
# earth-titles-careers.lib and earth-spheres-first-jobs.lib: the first moment grows from the title's turning point, the
# second is a later stretch of the same title.
# Syntax: header of build.py. Rules: SPEC.md. Every moment carries sphere: <sphere>, every option the face it acts in
# (a cross option X>Y the means colour's face). ladder: is the rung the title sits on in its face's ladder; haunt:
# where the moment happens at a haunt among its regulars.
# Evenness: every one-colour option has chance 75 and every cross option 65, so each colour's mean is the same.
# Pairs (complementary calls; cross cycles s1 and s2 four times each, s3 and s4 three times each):
#   café or bar owner  GB (s1) / WUR (s2)     music teacher  UW (s3) / BRG (s4)      touring musician  RB (s1) / WUG (s2)
#   folk musician      GR (s3) / WUB (s4)     craft maker    GU (s1) / WBR (s2)      independent preacher  BR (s3) / WUG (s4)
#   complementary therapist  BG (s1) / WUR (s2)
# Safety: the therapist's moments never offer or reward a remedy in place of a doctor; every act there keeps the
# sessions beside medicine. The preacher's hall names no real faith. holds: names titles not yet in
# earth_perks_titles.py (they wait for NEW_TITLES to be merged).

# ================================================================ café or bar owner (gather.G)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls GB (cycle s1); partner: the cold room that failed overnight

== the widower at the window table
tier: everyday
sphere: gather
haunt: gather.house
ladder: pillar
rate: 0.5
share: .003
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 21-80
calls: GB
holds: café or bar owner
tone: trouble
life: work, community
horizon: moment
roles: partner
earth: the old regular who has had the window table every morning for twenty years now sits there all day over one cup of tea, and a queue waits at the door
times: Most owners of a café with regulars, once or twice in their years there; small cafés live on turning tables at the busy hours, and regulars who stay all day are a common worry (estimate)
gap: 1-3
scene: It is the lunchtime rush at {Ns} café, and a queue waits at the door. The old man at the window table has come every morning for twenty years. Since his wife died, he stays all day over one cup of tea.
scene W: {N} opened the café so that anyone could come in and sit. A widower with nowhere else to go is exactly who it is for.
scene U: {N} knows the numbers: the window table at lunch is a fifth of the day's takings, and it has taken one cup of tea since nine.
scene B: The rent went up in the spring, and {partner} has been over the books twice this week. A table that earns nothing at the busiest hour hurts.
scene R: The queue is restless, someone at the door is tutting, and {N} is sick of feeling guilty in their own café.
scene G: {N} served the old man and his wife their anniversary cake at that table. The café is part of his week, and he is part of it.
- W | keep the window table his, rush or no rush, and work the queue faster | v: benevolence, universalism | sphere: gather.W | chance: 75
- U | move him to the quiet corner table, with a fresh pot on the house | v: benevolence, achievement | sphere: gather.U | chance: 75
- B | ask him kindly to come back after two, when the lunch crowd has gone | v: security, achievement | sphere: gather.B | chance: 75
- R | sit down with him for five minutes and let the queue wait | v: benevolence, hedonism | sphere: gather.R | chance: 75
- G | ask the other morning regulars to take turns keeping him company | v: benevolence, tradition | sphere: gather.G | chance: 75
- W>U | put up a sign saying tables are shared at lunch, and everyone is welcome | v: universalism, conformity | sphere: gather.W | chance: 65
- U>B | work out a cheap lunch deal for him, so the table earns its keep | v: achievement, benevolence | sphere: gather.U | chance: 65
- B>R | strike a deal with him: free tea for life, at the counter at lunchtimes | v: power, hedonism | sphere: gather.B | chance: 65
- R>G | tell the queue loudly that the window table is his, and always will be | v: tradition, stimulation | sphere: gather.R | chance: 65
- G>W | put his name on a small card on the window table, as the old cafés did | v: tradition, benevolence | sphere: gather.G | chance: 65
works: The lunch rush is served, and the old man is still at the window when {N} locks up.
works: Word of it gets round the street, and the café is busier than ever that month.
fails: The old man stops coming, and {N} hears that he now sits at home all day.
fails: Two lunch groups give up on the queue, and the week's takings come up short.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls WUR (cycle s2); partner: the widower at the window table

== the cold room that failed overnight
tier: everyday
sphere: gather
haunt: gather.house
ladder: pillar
rate: 0.5
share: .003
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 21-80
calls: WUR
holds: café or bar owner
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: moment
roles: partner
earth: you open the café at six to find the cold room failed overnight, and a wedding party of sixty sits down at noon
times: Most café and bar owners who serve food, once in their years there; cold-room failures are a common trouble of small kitchens, and food inspectors read the temperature logs (estimate)
gap: 2-6
scene: At six {N} opens the café and finds the cold room silent and warm: it failed in the night. A wedding party of sixty sits down at noon, and the meat, the cream and the cheese were all inside.
scene W: The rules are plain: food that sat warm for hours goes in the bin. The couple are owed the truth either way.
scene U: The thermometer reads eleven degrees. If the log shows when it failed, some of the food may still be safe, and some surely is not.
scene B: Sixty covers is the best booking of the year. Throwing out the stock and refunding the deposit could sink the month.
scene R: There are six hours. {N} could still buy everything fresh and cook the lot, with no sleep and no help.
scene G: The couple's grandparents had their own wedding lunch here, under the old owner. The family chose the café for that.
- W | bin everything that sat warm, and ring the couple to tell them now | v: conformity, universalism | sphere: gather.W | chance: 75
- U | check the cold room's log and keep only what the rules say is safe | v: security, achievement | sphere: gather.U | chance: 75
- B | serve whatever looks and smells fine, and keep the night's failure to yourself | act: serve whatever looks and smells fine, and keep the night's failure to themselves | v: security, power | closed: approval: serving food that sat warm all night; backfire: guests fall ill and the inspector reads the log | mark: hid a wrong | sphere: gather.B | chance: 75
- R | race to the wholesale market and cook the whole menu fresh by noon | v: stimulation, achievement | sphere: gather.R | chance: 75
- G | ring round family and regulars to cook a dish each for the party | v: benevolence, tradition | sphere: gather.G | chance: 75
- W>B | tell the couple honestly and offer a cut price for a smaller menu | v: universalism, security | sphere: gather.W | chance: 65
- U>R | plan a new menu from the dry stores and the bakery, and cook it fast | v: achievement, stimulation | sphere: gather.U | chance: 65
- B>G | call in a favour from the restaurant across the square for its cold room | v: security, benevolence | sphere: gather.B | chance: 65
- R>W | greet the couple at the door, tell them straight, and make it right | v: universalism, self-direction | sphere: gather.R | chance: 65
- G>U | ask the old owner, who ran this kitchen for thirty years, how he once coped | v: tradition | sphere: gather.G | chance: 65
works: The party sits down at half past twelve, and nobody at the long table guesses the morning {N} has had.
works: The couple send a card of thanks that stays up behind the counter for years.
fails: Lunch is late and thin, and the couple's family leave a cold review online.
fails: The week's stock goes in the bin, and {N} and {partner} spend a hard evening over the books.

# ================================================================ music teacher (arts.U)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls UW (cycle s3); partner: the best pupil and the band

== the pupil who cannot keep the beat
tier: everyday
sphere: arts
ladder: known
rate: 0.5
share: .0015
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-80
calls: UW
holds: music teacher
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: months
roles: mentor
earth: a pupil has practised every day for a year and still cannot keep the beat, and the parents ask whether another term is worth it
times: Most music teachers, a few times over their years; some pupils find timing hard for a long while, and parents weigh the fees term by term (estimate)
gap: 1-3
scene: A pupil of {Ns} has practised every day for a year and still cannot keep the beat. After the lesson the parents wait in the hall and ask whether another term is worth paying for.
scene W: {N} promised every pupil a fair chance and an honest word. The parents have paid for a year and deserve both.
scene U: {N} has watched closely: the child hears the beat but rushes whenever a passage gets hard. That can be taught.
scene B: The family pay on time every term. Their child is a small, steady part of what keeps {Ns} teaching going.
scene R: The child plays with real joy when nobody is counting out loud. The counting is what kills it.
scene G: {mentor}, who taught {N} as a child, used to say that every slow learner in the town band got there in the end.
- W | tell the parents plainly how far the child has come, and how far is left | v: universalism, conformity | sphere: arts.W | chance: 75
- U | teach the beat a new way: clapping, then walking, then the drum first | v: achievement, self-direction | sphere: arts.U | chance: 75
- B | recommend double lessons for a term, at the double fee | v: achievement, power | sphere: arts.B | chance: 75
- R | put the metronome away and let the child play along to a favourite song | v: hedonism, stimulation | sphere: arts.R | chance: 75
- G | bring the child to the town band's rehearsal to feel the beat among other players | v: tradition, benevolence | sphere: arts.G | chance: 75
- W>R | set a small end-of-term concert as a goal for the child to aim at | v: stimulation, conformity | sphere: arts.W | chance: 65
- U>G | find out what the family sings at home, and teach the beat through that | v: benevolence, tradition | sphere: arts.U | chance: 65
- B>W | offer the next term cheaper on one condition: a practice log signed every night | v: conformity, security | sphere: arts.B | chance: 65
- R>U | play fast duets with the child, quicker each week, until the beat sticks | v: stimulation, achievement | sphere: arts.R | chance: 65
- G>B | introduce the parents to an old pupil who struggled the same way and went far | v: tradition, achievement | sphere: arts.G | chance: 65
works: By the spring the child keeps time well enough to play in the end-of-term concert.
works: The parents book another term, and the child starts arriving early for lessons.
fails: The parents stop the lessons, and the child's instrument goes up for sale.
fails: Another term passes with no change, and {N} starts to dread the Tuesday lesson.

