horizon: months
roles: friend
earth: the biggest giver in your hall, who pays most of the rent, asks for a seat at the front and a say in next month's sermons
times: Most preachers with a hall of their own, once or more; small independent congregations often rest on a few large givers (estimate)
gap: 2-6
scene: {N} preaches in an old shop turned into a hall, with sixty chairs and a lease that runs to the spring. The biggest giver, who pays most of the rent, asks for a seat at the front and a say in next month's sermons.
scene W: {N} started the hall so that everyone in it would stand equal. A seat bought at the front goes against that.
scene U: {N} has done the sums: without this giver, the hall can pay its rent for four months.
scene B: The hall is {Ns} own work, built from nothing. A giver who steers the sermons steers the hall.
scene R: Every sermon {N} has given came from the heart, unplanned. Nobody tells {N} what to preach.
scene G: The giver's family has come every week since the first meeting, and their children were named in this hall.
- W | thank the giver, and say that every chair in the hall is equal | v: universalism | sphere: faith.W | chance: 75
- U | show the members the hall's accounts and ask how to share the rent | v: universalism, security | sphere: faith.U | chance: 75
